      "id": 1,
      "text": "Russell became its own town in 1792, breaking away from Westfield's 'New Addition' section.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofrussell.us/200/History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers clustered around Hazard Pond—later Russell Pond—whose water powered the first mills and sparked community growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Russell,_Massachusetts",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1841 railroad through the Berkshire Hills reshaped Russell, opening industrial access to abundant waterpower.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofrussell.us/200/History",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Woronoco grew into a company town when the Strathmore Paper Company, led by Horace A., set up a fine‑art paper mill there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Russell,_Massachusetts",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A prominent tannery in Russell village once employed many locals, feeding two whip‑lash factories that produced harnesses and lashes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Russell,_Massachusetts",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In the 1990s Mennonites from western Pennsylvania settled in Russell, opening a church, a furniture showroom called Country Woodcraft, and a bakery named Bread Basket.",
